    </article>

    <!-- Closing thesis -->
    <div class="closing">
      <p class="statement">Everything in the system is an abject. And I do mean everything.</p>
      <div class="ruled">
        <p>We don't need a special protocol between agents and software. We don't need a special protocol between agents and agents. We just need objects that can talk.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Put programs, LLMs, and peer-to-peer identity inside the same objects and you get something none of them could be alone. Biology has a word for that: symbiogenesis. And the objects can draw themselves. An X11-style Canvas compositor gives every abject its own window. No agent framework I know of gives its agents windows.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="defn"><span><em>symbiogenesis</em> (n.) - two organisms merging into a new form. It's how you got your mitochondria, and that worked out fine for you.</span></p>
    </div>
